Z||000002||Gold-86SE||02-23-2001||05:55 PM||||<BR>if you end up buying the fiero tomorrow an 85 is much better for an engine conversion than an 84.<P>just look for the standard stuff, when buying used.  also, look at the fiero specific parts, and what will need replacement.  keep a running total in your head, and determine whether or not it is worth your time and money.  and sometimes parts cars are worth their weight when only a couple hundred bucks.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||02-25-2001||12:06 PM||||Well i got it, it needs tires and a paint job, seats look brand new, no stains or anything..... what tire sizes should i get for it for year round (cheap ones though)<P>the gas pettal though sticks (idles at 2k), but if i put my foot behind the gas pettle and pull up it drops to 1k    and also it doesnnt start working until the petal is like half way down (well my 84 auto did that too, fiero thing?)<P>other then that i like it,   We did about 80 mph for about 1.5 hours bringing it home,  used barly any gas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  and this one spins the tires, not good though   but the auto didnt hehe||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||02-25-2001||03:30 PM||||Use some <B>WD-40</B> on the throttle linkage. My 84 SE used to stick. I just spayed <B>WD</B> on all the moving parts connecting to the throttle body. <P>Any pics of your new ride?<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
